A sparse input may encode a palette/order and phase for a periodic output, rather than a local fill operation. Read the output demo as a 2D sequence: identify the minimal period, row-to-row phase shift, and top-left phase. Verify exact candidate mappings cellwise; common alternatives include `(r+c) mod p` and `(c-r) mod p`, which may look alike but diverge on asymmetric patterns.

Do not transfer a periodic sequence from one compelling demo unless the same role-level rule reproduces every example. No-op pairs and outputs with apparently different palettes/periods are evidence that the operation may be conditional or that the current abstraction is wrong. Following an incorrect submission, binary feedback cannot pinpoint phase versus palette errors. Avoid arbitrary variant cycling; request a discriminating demo while available, and if capped, make one best-supported choice rather than repeat rejected outputs.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/periodic-pattern-transfer.md"}

